  </Fragment>

  <LifecycleNotice
    type="active"
    explanation="IPSAS 1 is the current, active Presentation of Financial Statements standard issued by the IPSASB. It is effective for annual financial statements covering periods beginning on or after 1 January 2008 (earlier application encouraged). The 2000 version was superseded by the current standard (para. 155, Withdrawal); first-time-adoption transitional relief formerly carried directly by IPSAS 1 now resides in IPSAS 33."
    link=""
    linkText=""
  />

      <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Objective &amp; Scope</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Objective – why IPSAS 1 exists</div>
        <div class="principle">To prescribe the manner in which general purpose financial statements should be presented, to ensure comparability both with the entity's own financial statements of previous periods and with the financial statements of other entities. It sets overall considerations for presentation, guidance for structure, and minimum content requirements for financial statements prepared under the accrual basis.</div>
        <div class="elabel">WHAT IPSAS 1 DOES NOT COVER</div>
        <div class="cross-list">
          <span>Recognition, measurement, or disclosure of specific transactions and events – governed by the relevant topic Standard (e.g. IPSAS 45, IPSAS 12), not IPSAS 1</span>
        </div>
        <div class="note">IPSAS 1 is almost entirely a presentation standard, paras. 1–155, all of equal authority – read together with the Preface to IPSAS and the Conceptual Framework.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Scope – who applies it, and to what</div>
        <div class="elabel">APPLIES TO</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span>All general purpose financial statements prepared and presented under the accrual basis of accounting in accordance with IPSAS</span>
          <span>Entities presenting consolidated financial statements (IPSAS 35) and those presenting separate financial statements (IPSAS 34) – equally</span>
          <span>Statements presented separately or within another public document (e.g. an annual report)</span>
        </div>
        <div class="elabel">DOES NOT APPLY TO</div>
        <div class="cross-list">
          <span>Condensed interim financial information</span>
          <span>Cash-basis reporting – governed by the separate Cash Basis IPSAS</span>
        </div>
        <div class="callout info callout--flush"><strong>Who is a "user"?</strong> Taxpayers/ratepayers, members of the legislature, creditors, suppliers, the media, and employees – people not in a position to demand tailored reports. This is why IPSAS 1 optimises for broad comparability, not any one stakeholder's bespoke needs.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Definitions – Core Terms (para. 7, 7A)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Key definitions you must know</div>
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Accrual basis</div><div class="glossary-def">Transactions and events are recognized when they OCCUR – not only when cash or its equivalent is received or paid. Elements recognized: assets, liabilities, net assets/equity, revenue, expenses.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Assets</div><div class="glossary-def">Resources controlled by an entity as a result of past events, from which future economic benefits or service potential are expected to flow to the entity.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Liabilities</div><div class="glossary-def">Present obligations arising from past events, the settlement of which is expected to result in an outflow of resources embodying economic benefits or service potential.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item glossary-item--last"><div class="glossary-term">Net assets/equity</div><div class="glossary-def">The residual interest in the assets of the entity after deducting all its liabilities – may be positive or negative. Components: contributed capital, accumulated surpluses/deficits, reserves, non-controlling interests.</div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cn">
        <div class="ctitle cn">More core definitions</div>
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Revenue</div><div class="glossary-def">Gross inflow of economic benefits or service potential during the reporting period, when it increases net assets/equity, other than increases relating to contributions from owners.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Expenses</div><div class="glossary-def">Decreases in economic benefits or service potential in the form of outflows/consumption of assets or incurrence of liabilities, that decrease net assets/equity – other than distributions to owners.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Contributions / distributions to owners</div><div class="glossary-def">Future economic benefits or service potential contributed BY, or distributed TO, owners in their capacity as owners – establishing or realising a financial interest in net assets/equity.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item glossary-item--last"><div class="glossary-term">Impracticable</div><div class="glossary-def">A requirement is impracticable when the entity cannot apply it after making every reasonable effort to do so.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="grid grid-spaced">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Terms imported from IPSAS 28 (para. 7A) &amp; the full "Notes" definition</div>
        <div class="two-col">
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">PUTTABLE INSTRUMENTS (IPSAS 28)</div>
            <div class="dot-list">
              <span>A puttable financial instrument that IPSAS 28 nonetheless permits to be classified as an equity instrument, subject to specified conditions</span>
              <span>An instrument obliging the entity to deliver a pro rata share of net assets to another party ONLY ON LIQUIDATION, also classified as equity under IPSAS 28</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">NOTES</div>
            <div class="text-supporting">Information additional to that presented in the SOFP, Statement of Financial Performance, Statement of Changes in Net Assets/Equity, and Cash Flow Statement – narrative descriptions, disaggregations, and information about items not qualifying for recognition.</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="note">Terms defined in other IPSAS carry the same meaning here, and are reproduced in the IPSASB's separately published Glossary of Defined Terms. See section 22 for the complete Quick-Reference Glossary, including net assets/equity reserve types.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Economic Entity (paras. 8–14)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci">
        <div class="ctitle ci">What "economic entity" means</div>
        <div class="principle">A controlling entity and its controlled entities, used for financial reporting purposes. Other terms sometimes used: administrative entity, financial entity, consolidated entity, group.</div>
        <div class="worked worked--flush">
          <div class="wk-title">Worked illustration</div>
          <div class="wk-body">A government housing department may be an economic entity that includes controlled entities providing housing at a nominal charge (social policy objective) alongside controlled entities providing accommodation on a commercial basis.</div>
          <div class="wk-answer">Both sit inside the same economic entity for consolidation purposes – social and commercial objectives can coexist within one reporting group.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">4.1–4.4 – the explanatory cluster following the core definitions</div>
        <div class="spec-box">
          <div class="spec-title">4.1 Future economic benefits or service potential</div>
          <div class="text-supporting">Service potential = assets used to deliver goods/services per the entity's objectives without directly generating net cash inflows (e.g. a school, a road). Future economic benefits = assets used to generate net cash inflows. IPSAS 1 uses the composite phrase to capture both.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="spec-box">
          <div class="spec-title">4.2 Government Business Enterprises</div>
          <div class="text-supporting"><em>[Deleted]</em> – para. 12 and the related definition were removed by <em>The Applicability of IPSAS</em> (Apr 2016). "GBE" no longer has a defined role in IPSAS 1.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="spec-box">
          <div class="spec-title">4.3 Materiality</div>
          <div class="text-supporting">Users are assumed to have reasonable knowledge of the public sector, economic activities, and accounting, and a willingness to study information with reasonable diligence – the materiality test is framed around that user.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="spec-box">
          <div class="spec-title">4.4 Net assets/equity</div>
          <div class="formula-box">
            <div class="f-eq">Net Assets/Equity = Assets − Liabilities</div>
          </div>
          <div class="text-supporting">The residual measure on the SOFP – may be positive or negative. Other terms (e.g. "equity", "taxpayers' equity") may be substituted provided the meaning is clear.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Purpose &amp; Responsibility for Financial Statements (paras. 15–20)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Purpose – accountability + decision-usefulness</div>
        <div class="principle">Financial statements are a structured representation of financial position and performance. In the public sector, general purpose financial reporting exists to inform decisions AND demonstrate accountability for resources entrusted to the entity.</div>
        <div class="elabel">FIVE ACCOUNTABILITY OBJECTIVES (PARA. 15)</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span>Sources, allocation, and uses of financial resources</span>
          <span>How the entity financed its activities and met cash requirements</span>
          <span>Ability to finance activities and meet liabilities/commitments</span>
          <span>Financial condition and changes in it</span>
          <span>Aggregate performance – service costs, efficiency, accomplishments</span>
        </div>
        <div class="callout info callout--flush"><strong>Financial statements also have a predictive role</strong> – indicating resources needed for continued operations, and whether resources were obtained/used per the legally adopted budget and legal/contractual limits.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Responsibility for preparation &amp; presentation</div>
        <div class="two-col">
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">INDIVIDUAL ENTITY LEVEL</div>
            <div class="text-supporting">Typically the head of the entity (permanent head/chief executive) and the head of the central finance agency (controller/accountant-general). Jurisdictions may split "prepares" from "approves/presents."</div>
          </div>
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">WHOLE-OF-GOVERNMENT LEVEL</div>
            <div class="text-supporting">Responsibility for consolidated statements usually rests jointly with the head of the central finance agency and the finance minister (or equivalent).</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="note">Financial statements are unlikely to meet every accountability objective alone, since public sector entities are typically not profit-driven – supplementary information (incl. non-financial statements) may accompany them (para. 18).</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>A Complete Set of Financial Statements (paras. 21–26)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs">
        <div class="ctitle cs">The seven required components</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span>A statement of financial position</span>
          <span>A statement of financial performance</span>
          <span>A statement of changes in net assets/equity</span>
          <span>A cash flow statement</span>
          <span>A budget-vs-actual comparison – when the entity's approved budget is publicly available (IPSAS 24)</span>
          <span>Notes – summary of significant accounting policies plus other explanatory notes</span>
          <span>Comparative information for the preceding period (para. 53, 53A)</span>
        </div>
        <div class="note">Alternate names in use across jurisdictions: SOFP = "balance sheet"/"statement of assets and liabilities"; statement of financial performance = "income statement"/"operating statement"/"P&amp;L"; notes may include items called "schedules."</div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Budgetary accountability &amp; encouraged extras</div>
        <div class="elabel">BUDGET COMPARISON</div>
        <div class="text-supporting">Mandatory (IPSAS 24) where the budget is publicly available. Otherwise IPSAS 1 encourages a columnar budget-vs-actual comparison (with an optional variance column) or a disclosure that budgeted amounts were not exceeded.</div>
        <div class="elabel">ENCOURAGED SUPPLEMENTARY REPORTING</div>
        <div class="dot-list">
          <span>Performance indicators &amp; statements of service performance</span>
          <span>Program reviews and management commentary on achievements</span>
          <span>Compliance with legislative/regulatory/other externally-imposed requirements</span>
        </div>
        <div class="callout warn callout--flush">Public sector entities are typically subject to appropriations or budget authorizations – non-compliance disclosure is relevant to accountability and may influence future resource-allocation decisions.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Fair Presentation &amp; Compliance with IPSAS (paras. 27–37)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">The compliance package &amp; the "extremely rare" override</div>
        <div class="principle">Financial statements shall present fairly financial position, financial performance, and cash flows. Fair presentation = faithful representation of transactions per IPSAS definitions and recognition criteria. Applying IPSAS, with extra disclosure when necessary, is presumed to achieve fair presentation.</div>
        <div class="two-col">
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">AN ENTITY CLAIMING COMPLIANCE MUST</div>
            <div class="check-list">
              <span>Make an explicit, unreserved statement of compliance in the notes (para. 28)</span>
              <span>Comply with all requirements – no partial-compliance labelling</span>
              <span>Select/apply policies per IPSAS 3's hierarchy where no Standard specifically applies</span>
              <span>Provide extra disclosure whenever specific compliance is insufficient for understanding</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">WHAT DOES NOT CURE A PROBLEM</div>
            <div class="cross-list">
              <span>Inappropriate accounting policies are not rectified by disclosure, notes, or explanatory material (para. 30)</span>
            </div>
            <div class="elabel">NOT AN ESCAPE HATCH</div>
            <div class="cross-list">
              <span>Departures made purely to satisfy statutory/legislative reporting requirements do not qualify under para. 31 – if material, that's simply non-compliance (para. 37)</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="callout danger callout--flush"><strong>Extremely rare override (paras. 31–37):</strong> where compliance would be so misleading it conflicts with the objective of financial statements, AND the regulatory framework PERMITS departure, the entity shall depart – disclosing the fair-presentation statement, compliance-except-for statement, the departed requirement &amp; reason, and the financial impact per period. If the framework prohibits departure, the entity instead discloses the requirement, why it misleads, and the adjustments that would have achieved fair presentation.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Going Concern &amp; Consistency of Presentation (paras. 38–44)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Going concern – assessed, not assumed</div>
        <div class="principle">Management assesses the entity's ability to continue as a going concern. Statements are prepared on that basis unless there's an intention to liquidate, cease operating, or no realistic alternative. Material uncertainties casting significant doubt must be disclosed even if the going-concern conclusion still holds.</div>
        <div class="callout warn callout--flush">Public sector going concern is different: a government's power to levy taxes or rates may support going concern even with extended periods of negative net assets/equity. The test is primarily relevant at the individual entity level, not whole-of-government – multi-year funding agreements can support going concern even where an entity's own SOFP looks weak.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cn">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Consistency of presentation</div>
        <div class="principle">Presentation and classification is retained period to period unless (a) a significant change in operations, or a review of the statements, shows another presentation/classification is more appropriate under IPSAS 3's criteria, or (b) an IPSAS requires the change.</div>
        <div class="text-supporting">A change is only made when it's faithfully representative AND more relevant, and the revised structure is likely to continue (so comparability isn't impaired). Comparatives are then reclassified under paras. 55–56.</div>
        <div class="note">A significant acquisition/disposal, or a structural review, is the typical trigger – e.g. disposing of a controlled entity that changes the economic entity's principal activity.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Materiality, Aggregation &amp; Offsetting (paras. 45–52)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Materiality &amp; aggregation</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span>Each material class of similar items presented separately</span>
          <span>Dissimilar items presented separately unless immaterial</span>
          <span>Immaterial line items aggregated on the face or in the notes</span>
          <span>An item immaterial for face presentation may still warrant separate note disclosure</span>
        </div>
        <div class="note">A specific IPSAS disclosure requirement need not be satisfied if the information is immaterial (para. 47).</div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cd">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Offsetting – general prohibition, narrow exceptions</div>
        <div class="callout danger">Assets and liabilities, and revenue and expenses, shall not be offset unless required or permitted by an IPSAS – offsetting hides users' ability to understand transactions and assess future cash flows.</div>
        <div class="two-col">
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">NOT OFFSETTING</div>
            <div class="dot-list"><span>Measuring assets net of valuation allowances – e.g. obsolescence, doubtful debts</span></div>
          </div>
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">PERMITTED NETTING (SUBSTANCE-BASED)</div>
            <div class="dot-list">
              <span>Gains/losses on disposal of non-current assets</span>
              <span>Provision expenses reimbursed under contract (e.g. warranty)</span>
              <span>Gains/losses from a group of similar transactions – unless material</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Comparative Information (paras. 53–58)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Minimum comparative information (para. 53A)</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span>1 × Statement of financial position, with comparative period</span>
          <span>1 × Statement of financial performance, with comparative period</span>
          <span>1 × Cash flow statement, with comparative period</span>
          <span>1 × Statement of changes in net assets/equity, with comparative period</span>
          <span>Related notes</span>
        </div>
        <div class="note">Comparative narrative/descriptive information is also required where relevant to understanding the current period (e.g. an unresolved legal dispute carried forward).</div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Reclassification of comparatives</div>
        <div class="principle">When presentation/classification is amended, comparatives are reclassified unless impracticable.</div>
        <div class="two-col">
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">IF RECLASSIFIED – DISCLOSE</div>
            <div class="dot-list"><span>The nature of the reclassification</span><span>The amount of each item/class reclassified</span><span>The reason for the reclassification</span></div>
          </div>
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">IF IMPRACTICABLE – DISCLOSE</div>
            <div class="dot-list"><span>Why the amounts were not reclassified</span><span>The nature of the adjustments that would have been made</span></div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="note">IPSAS 3 governs the adjustments to comparatives required for a change in accounting policy or correction of an error.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Statement of Financial Position – Structure (paras. 88–98)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Minimum line items on the face (para. 88)</div>
        <div class="two-col">
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">ASSETS</div>
            <div class="dot-list">
              <span>Property, plant &amp; equipment</span>
              <span>Investment property</span>
              <span>Intangible assets</span>
              <span>Financial assets</span>
              <span>Investments (equity method)</span>
              <span>Inventories</span>
              <span>Receivables</span>
              <span>Cash and cash equivalents</span>
              <span>Assets held for sale (IPSAS 44)</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">LIABILITIES &amp; NET ASSETS/EQUITY</div>
            <div class="dot-list">
              <span>Taxes &amp; transfers payable</span>
              <span>Social benefits liabilities</span>
              <span>Payables</span>
              <span>Provisions</span>
              <span>Financial liabilities</span>
              <span>Liabilities in disposal groups held for sale</span>
              <span>Non-controlling interest, within net assets/equity</span>
              <span>Net assets/equity attributable to owners of controlling entity</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="note">IPSAS 1 does not prescribe order or format – para. 88 lists items sufficiently different in nature/function to warrant separate presentation. Different measurement models for asset classes (e.g. cost vs revalued PP&amp;E under IPSAS 45) itself signals separate line presentation.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cd">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Face-or-notes subclassifications &amp; no-share-capital disclosure</div>
        <div class="elabel">TYPICAL SUBCLASSIFICATIONS (PARA. 94)</div>
        <div class="dot-list">
          <span>PP&amp;E disaggregated into classes (IPSAS 45)</span>
          <span>Receivables split: user charges, taxes/other revenue, related parties, prepayments, other</span>
          <span>Inventories per IPSAS 12: merchandise, supplies, materials, WIP, finished goods</span>
          <span>Social benefits liabilities by scheme, where material</span>
        </div>
        <div class="elabel">NO SHARE CAPITAL – DISCLOSE SEPARATELY (PARA. 95)</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span>Contributed capital (cumulative contributions less distributions)</span>
          <span>Accumulated surpluses or deficits</span>
          <span>Reserves, with nature &amp; purpose of each</span>
          <span>Non-controlling interests</span>
        </div>
        <div class="note">Most public sector entities lack share capital and are controlled exclusively by another public sector entity – the government's interest is a blend of contributed capital and accumulated surpluses/reserves (para. 96). A non-controlling interest can still arise, e.g. a partly-privatized commercial public sector entity within the economic entity.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Current / Non-Current Classification (paras. 70–87C)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Current asset – any one of four tests (para. 76)</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span>Expected to be realized, or held for sale/consumption, in the normal operating cycle</span>
          <span>Held primarily for the purpose of trading</span>
          <span>Expected to be realized within 12 months of the reporting date</span>
          <span>Is cash or a cash equivalent, unless restricted from exchange/use to settle a liability for ≥12 months</span>
        </div>
        <div class="note">All other assets → non-current. Where the operating cycle isn't clearly identifiable, it's assumed to be 12 months – so taxes/user-charges receivable, inventories, and accrued investment revenue stay current even if realized beyond 12 months.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Current liability – any one of four tests (para. 80)</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span>Expected to be settled in the normal operating cycle</span>
          <span>Held primarily for the purpose of trading</span>
          <span>Due to be settled within 12 months of the reporting date</span>
          <span>The entity does not have the right, at the reporting date, to defer settlement for ≥12 months</span>
        </div>
        <div class="note">All other liabilities → non-current. A mixed presentation basis (current/non-current for some items, liquidity order for others) is permitted where an entity has diverse operations and it's more relevant (para. 74).</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="grid grid-spaced">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">2023 amendment cluster – the "right to defer" tightened (paras. 83A–87C, effective 1 Jan 2026, retrospective)</div>
        <div class="principle"><em>Improvements to IPSAS, 2023</em> sharpened para. 80(d): the right to defer settlement for 12 months must have substance and must exist at the reporting date.</div>
        <div class="two-col">
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">LOAN COVENANTS – DO THEY AFFECT THE RIGHT?</div>
            <div class="dot-list">
              <span>Covenant tested on or before the reporting date → affects the right (even if compliance is only assessed afterwards)</span>
              <span>Covenant tested only after the reporting date (e.g. 6 months later) → does not affect the right at the reporting date – but triggers disclosure under para. 87A</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">CLASSIFICATION IS UNAFFECTED BY</div>
            <div class="dot-list">
              <span>Management's intention or expectation to settle early</span>
              <span>Actual early settlement between the reporting date and authorization for issue (para. 86A)</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="worked worked--flush">
          <div class="wk-title">Worked example – covenant breach at year end</div>
          <div class="wk-body">An entity breaches a long-term loan covenant on or before the reporting date, making the liability payable on demand. The lender agrees after the reporting date (but before authorization for issue) not to demand payment.</div>
          <div class="wk-answer">The liability is still classified as <strong class="text-danger">CURRENT</strong> – at the reporting date itself, the entity lacked the right to defer settlement for 12 months. Exception: if the lender had, by the reporting date, already granted a grace period ending ≥12 months after the reporting date, the liability is <strong class="text-success">non-current</strong>.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="grid grid-spaced">
      <div class="card ci">
        <div class="ctitle ci">"Settlement" now has a precise meaning (paras. 87B–87C)</div>
        <div class="text-supporting">A transfer to the counterparty that extinguishes the liability: cash, other resources (goods/services), or the entity's own equity instruments – unless a counterparty-option conversion feature is separately classified as equity under IPSAS 28.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cn">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Non-adjusting events (para. 87, cross-ref IPSAS 14)</div>
        <div class="dot-list">
          <span>Refinancing a current liability on a long-term basis</span>
          <span>Rectifying a covenant breach, or receiving a grace period to do so</span>
          <span>Settlement of a liability classified as non-current</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Statement of Financial Performance (paras. 99–117)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Minimum line items on the face (para. 102)</div>
        <div class="dot-list">
          <span>Revenue – separately: interest revenue (effective interest method); gains/losses on derecognition of amortized-cost financial assets</span>
          <span>Finance costs</span>
          <span>Impairment losses/reversals (IPSAS 41)</span>
          <span>Share of surplus/deficit of associates &amp; joint ventures (equity method)</span>
          <span>Gain/loss on reclassification between FV categories (IPSAS 41)</span>
          <span>Surplus or deficit</span>
          <span>Total of discontinued operations (single amount, IPSAS 44)</span>
        </div>
        <div class="note">Also shown as allocations of surplus/deficit: amounts attributable to non-controlling interest, and to owners of the controlling entity (para. 103).</div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Expense analysis – two permitted methods</div>
        <div class="elabel">POLICY CHOICE – WHICHEVER IS MORE RELEVANT</div>
        <div class="dot-list">
          <span><strong>By nature</strong> – e.g. employee benefits, depreciation, social benefits expense (simple; no functional allocation)</span>
          <span><strong>By function</strong> – e.g. health, education, social benefits expense (more relevant; needs judgment-heavy allocation)</span>
        </div>
        <div class="callout warn callout--flush"><strong>Function method carries a price:</strong> entities using the function method must additionally disclose the nature of expenses – including depreciation and amortization, social benefits expense, and employee benefits expense (para. 115) – since nature-based information is useful for predicting future cash flows.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="grid grid-spaced">
      <div class="card cs">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Illustrative extract – nature of expense method</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:2fr 1fr;"><span>Line item</span><span>20X2</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:2fr 1fr;"><span>Revenue</span><span>X</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:2fr 1fr;"><span>Employee benefits costs</span><span>(X)</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:2fr 1fr;"><span>Social benefits expenses</span><span>(X)</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:2fr 1fr;"><span>Depreciation &amp; amortization</span><span>(X)</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:2fr 1fr;"><span>Other expenses</span><span>(X)</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:2fr 1fr;"><span><strong>Surplus</strong></span><span><strong>X</strong></span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cd">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Illustrative extract – function of expense method</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:2fr 1fr;"><span>Line item</span><span>20X2</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:2fr 1fr;"><span>Revenue</span><span>X</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:2fr 1fr;"><span>Social benefits expenses</span><span>(X)</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:2fr 1fr;"><span>Health expenses</span><span>(X)</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:2fr 1fr;"><span>Education expenses</span><span>(X)</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:2fr 1fr;"><span>Other expenses</span><span>(X)</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:2fr 1fr;"><span><strong>Surplus</strong></span><span><strong>X</strong></span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="grid grid-spaced">
      <div class="card cn span2">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Separate disclosure triggers (para. 107) &amp; dividends</div>
        <div class="dot-list">
          <span>Inventory write-downs to NRV, or PP&amp;E write-downs to recoverable amount, and reversals</span>
          <span>Restructurings and reversals of restructuring provisions</span>
          <span>Disposals of PP&amp;E; privatizations or other investment disposals</span>
          <span>Discontinued operations; litigation settlements; other provision reversals</span>
        </div>
        <div class="note">Where an entity has share capital and provides a dividend/similar distribution, it discloses the amount recognized as distributions to owners during the period, and the related amount per share – on the face of the SOFPerformance, the Statement of Changes in Net Assets/Equity, or in the notes (para. 117).</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Statement of Changes in Net Assets/Equity (paras. 118–125C)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Required content</div>
        <div class="elabel">ON THE FACE (PARA. 118)</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span>Surplus or deficit for the period</span>
          <span>Each item of revenue/expense recognized DIRECTLY in net assets/equity, and their total</span>
          <span>Total revenue &amp; expense for the period, split between owners and non-controlling interest</span>
          <span>Per component: effect of policy changes &amp; error corrections (IPSAS 3)</span>
        </div>
        <div class="elabel">FACE OR NOTES (PARA. 119)</div>
        <div class="dot-list">
          <span>Transactions with owners in their capacity as owners, distributions shown separately</span>
          <span>Full reconciliation of opening to closing carrying amount, per component, each change shown separately</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Reclassification adjustments</div>
        <div class="principle">Some amounts previously recognized directly in net assets/equity are later reclassified to surplus or deficit when a specified event occurs – e.g. disposal of a foreign operation (IPSAS 4), or specified hedged cash flows affecting surplus/deficit (IPSAS 41).</div>
        <div class="callout info callout--flush"><strong>No reclassification</strong> arises for revaluation surplus (IPSAS 45) or defined-benefit-plan remeasurements (IPSAS 39) – these stay in net assets/equity permanently, though revaluation surplus may transfer to accumulated surpluses/deficits as the asset is used or derecognized (para. 125C).</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Cash Flow Statement (para. 126)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="ctitle cs">A mandatory component IPSAS 1 doesn't itself govern</div>
        <div class="principle">Cash flow information gives users a basis to assess (a) the entity's ability to generate cash and cash equivalents, and (b) its needs to utilize those cash flows. IPSAS 1 only anchors the cash flow statement as a mandatory component of a complete set – the detailed presentation and disclosure requirements sit entirely in <strong>IPSAS 2</strong>, <em>Cash Flow Statements</em>.</div>
        <div class="note">The cash flow statement is the only primary statement not prepared under the accrual basis.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s15"><span class="num">15</span><h2>Notes – Structure, Accounting Policies &amp; Estimation Uncertainty (paras. 127–148)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Conventional note ordering (para. 129)</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span>Statement of compliance with IPSAS</span>
          <span>Summary of significant accounting policies</span>
          <span>Supporting information for face items, in statement/line-item order</span>
          <span>Other disclosures – contingent liabilities (IPSAS 19), unrecognized commitments, financial risk management (IPSAS 30)</span>
        </div>
        <div class="note">Deviating is acceptable where it aids understanding (e.g. combining fair-value-change disclosures with maturity disclosures) – but a systematic structure is retained as far as practicable.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="card ci">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Disclosure of accounting policies</div>
        <div class="dot-list">
          <span>Measurement basis/bases used (historical cost, current operational value, cost of fulfillment, fair value)</span>
          <span>Extent of transitional provisions applied under any IPSAS</span>
          <span>Other policies relevant to understanding the statements</span>
        </div>
        <div class="callout warn callout--flush"><strong>Judgments vs. estimates – often confused:</strong> para. 137 requires disclosure of JUDGMENTS (other than estimation) with the most significant effect on recognized amounts (e.g. is an arrangement in substance a lease?). This is distinct from the estimation-uncertainty disclosures at para. 140.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="grid grid-spaced">
      <div class="card cw span2">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Key sources of estimation uncertainty (paras. 140–148)</div>
        <div class="callout danger">Where key assumptions about the future, or other key sources of estimation uncertainty, carry a significant risk of causing a material adjustment to carrying amounts within the next financial year, the entity discloses their nature and carrying amount at the reporting date.</div>
        <div class="two-col">
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">TYPICAL TRIGGERS (PARA. 141)</div>
            <div class="dot-list">
              <span>Recoverable amount of certain PP&amp;E classes</span>
              <span>Technological obsolescence effects on inventories</span>
              <span>Provisions dependent on litigation outcomes</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">GOOD DISCLOSURE INCLUDES (PARA. 144)</div>
            <div class="dot-list">
              <span>The nature of the assumption/uncertainty</span>
              <span>Sensitivity of carrying amounts, and why</span>
              <span>Expected resolution &amp; range of reasonably possible outcomes within the next year</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="callout suc callout--flush"><strong>Carve-out:</strong> not required for assets/liabilities measured at current operational value or fair value based on a quoted price in an active market for an identical item (para. 143). Budget information/forecasts need not be disclosed to satisfy para. 140 (para. 145).</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s16"><span class="num">16</span><h2>Notes – Capital, Puttable Instruments &amp; Other Disclosures (paras. 148A–150)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Capital management disclosures (paras. 148A–148C)</div>
        <div class="dot-list">
          <span>Qualitative: what it manages as capital; externally imposed capital requirements &amp; how incorporated; how it is meeting objectives</span>
          <span>Quantitative: summary data on what it manages as capital</span>
          <span>Period-on-period changes in the above</span>
          <span>Whether it complied with externally imposed capital requirements – and consequences if not</span>
        </div>
        <div class="note">Based on information provided internally to key management personnel. Where aggregate disclosure across multiple capital regimes would distort understanding (e.g. a conglomerate spanning insurance and banking), separate disclosure per requirement is required instead.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Puttable instruments &amp; other disclosures</div>
        <div class="elabel">PUTTABLE INSTRUMENTS CLASSIFIED AS EQUITY (PARA. 148D)</div>
        <div class="dot-list">
          <span>Summary quantitative data on the amount classified within net assets/equity</span>
          <span>Objectives/policies/processes for managing the repurchase/redemption obligation</span>
          <span>Expected cash outflow on redemption, and how it was determined</span>
        </div>
        <div class="elabel">OTHER DISCLOSURES (PARAS. 149–150)</div>
        <div class="dot-list">
          <span>Dividends proposed/declared before authorization but not recognized, plus amount per share</span>
          <span>Domicile, legal form, jurisdiction, nature of operations, governing legislation</span>
          <span>Name of the controlling entity and the ultimate controlling entity</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s17"><span class="num">17</span><h2>Effective Date &amp; Amendment Timeline (paras. 151–155)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Amendment history at a glance</div>
        <div class="principle">Base Standard: periods beginning on/after <strong>1 January 2008</strong> (earlier application encouraged). Transitional provisions (former paras. 151–152) were deleted by IPSAS 33 – first-time-adoption relief now lives exclusively there. IPSAS 1 (2000) was superseded (para. 155, &quot;Withdrawal&quot;).</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2.2fr;"><span>Effective</span><span>Change</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2.2fr;"><span>1 Jan 2011</span><span>Paras. 79, 82 – Improvements to IPSAS (Jan 2010)</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2.2fr;"><span>1 Jan 2012</span><span>Para. 80 – Improvements to IPSAS (Nov 2010)</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2.2fr;"><span>1 Jan 2013</span><span>Paras. 150, 7A, 95A, 148D (IPSAS 28); paras. 75, 129, 148, 148A–148C (IPSAS 30)</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2.2fr;"><span>2015</span><span>Paras. 4, 7, 12, 88(n), 95(d), 97, 103, 118(c), 134, 135, 139 – IPSAS 35/38 suite</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2.2fr;"><span>1 Jan 2017</span><span>Paras. 29, 44, 70, 73, 74, 109, 116; Appendix A deleted – Improvements to IPSAS 2015</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2.2fr;"><span>1 Jan 2018</span><span>Paras. 5, 6, 12 deleted; paras. 7, 97, 116 – Applicability of IPSAS &amp; IPSAS 39</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2.2fr;"><span>1 Jan 2019</span><span>Para. 135 – IPSAS 40, Public Sector Combinations</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2.2fr;"><span>1 Jan 2023</span><span>Paras. 7, 79, 82, 101, 102, 138; new 125A–125C – IPSAS 41, Financial Instruments</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2.2fr;"><span>1 Jan 2025</span><span>Paras. 88, 102, 107 (IPSAS 44); paras. 7, 92, 94, 101, 125C, 134, 148 (IPSAS 45); paras. 133, 134, 141, 143 (IPSAS 46)</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2.2fr;"><span>1 Jan 2026</span><span>Paras. 50, 88, 94, 135 – IPSAS 47, Revenue</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2.2fr;"><span>1 Jan 2026, retrospective</span><span>New 83A, 83B, 86A, 87A–87C; amended 70, 80, 82, 84, 85, 87 – <strong>Improvements to IPSAS, 2023</strong> (current/non-current package, section 11)</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="callout warn callout--flush">Two separate amendment packages both bite for periods beginning on/after 1 January 2026 – IPSAS 47 and the Improvements to IPSAS, 2023 current/non-current liability package. Both belong on the same 2026 effective-date checklist.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s18"><span class="num">18</span><h2>Relationship to IAS/IFRS – Comparison with IAS 1</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Comparison – IPSAS 1 vs IAS 1</div>
        <div class="principle">IPSAS 1 is drawn primarily from <strong>IAS 1 (Revised 2003)</strong>, including the IASB's May 2008 &amp; April 2009 Improvements to IFRS. IPSASB policy is to converge accrual-basis IPSAS with IFRS where appropriate for public sector entities, departing only where IFRS requirements/terminology don't fit, or where extra commentary is needed.</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.1fr 1.3fr 1.3fr;"><span>Topic</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">IPSAS 1</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">IAS 1</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.1fr 1.3fr 1.3fr;"><span>Going concern</span><span>Public-sector-specific discussion (e.g. taxing power)</span><span>No equivalent discussion</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.1fr 1.3fr 1.3fr;"><span>Statement of changes in equity</span><span>REQUIRES a statement showing ALL changes in net assets/equity</span><span>Permits an all-changes statement, OR one excluding owner transactions</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.1fr 1.3fr 1.3fr;"><span>Statement names</span><span>"Statement of financial performance"; "net assets/equity"</span><span>"Income statement"/SOPL; "equity"</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.1fr 1.3fr 1.3fr;"><span>Income vs revenue</span><span>Uses the narrower "revenue" only</span><span>Uses the broader "income," which also captures gains</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.1fr 1.3fr 1.3fr;"><span>Responsibility for F/S</span><span>Contains commentary on who prepares/presents (paras. 19–20)</span><span>No equivalent commentary</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.1fr 1.3fr 1.3fr;"><span>Timeliness</span><span>Explicit commentary (para. 69) – no equivalent Framework existed historically</span><span>No equivalent explicit commentary</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.1fr 1.3fr 1.3fr;"><span>Extraordinary items</span><span>Does not explicitly preclude presenting them</span><span>Prohibits presenting any items as extraordinary</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="note">IPSAS 44 (2022) brought forward the IAS 1 amendments consequential on IFRS 5 that had previously been excluded pending an IPSASB view on public sector applicability – IPSAS 1 is now fully current with those changes too.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s19"><span class="num">19</span><h2>Statement Templates (Implementation Guidance IG1–IG4)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
        <div class="ctitle cn">How to use these templates</div>
        <div class="callout info callout--flush"><strong>These illustrate one way of meeting IPSAS 1's minimum requirements</strong>, drawn from the Standard's own Implementation Guidance (a national government example). IPSAS 1 does not prescribe order or exact line descriptions – adapt to each entity's circumstances. SOFPerformance templates (nature &amp; function methods) are shown in section 12; this section covers the SOFP, the Statement of Changes in Net Assets/Equity, and the accounting-policies extract.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="grid grid-spaced">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Statement of Financial Position – as at Dec 31, 20X2 (thousands of currency units)</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:2.4fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Line item</span><span>20X2</span><span>20X1</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:2.4fr 1fr 1fr;"><span><strong>ASSETS – Current</strong></span><span></span><span></span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:2.4fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Cash and cash equivalents</span><span>X</span><span>X</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:2.4fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Receivables</span><span>X</span><span>X</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:2.4fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Inventories, prepayments, other</span><span>X</span><span>X</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:2.4fr 1fr 1fr;"><span><strong>Total current assets</strong></span><span><strong>X</strong></span><span><strong>X</strong></span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:2.4fr 1fr 1fr;"><span><strong>Non-current</strong></span><span></span><span></span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:2.4fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Infrastructure, plant &amp; equipment; land &amp; buildings</span><span>X</span><span>X</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:2.4fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Investments in associates; other financial &amp; intangible assets</span><span>X</span><span>X</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:2.4fr 1fr 1fr;"><span><strong>Total assets</strong></span><span><strong>X</strong></span><span><strong>X</strong></span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:2.4fr 1fr 1fr;"><span><strong>LIABILITIES – Current</strong></span><span></span><span></span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:2.4fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Payables, short-term borrowings, provisions</span><span>X</span><span>X</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:2.4fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Social benefits, employee benefits, superannuation</span><span>X</span><span>X</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:2.4fr 1fr 1fr;"><span><strong>Non-current</strong> – long-term borrowings, provisions, social/employee benefits</span><span>X</span><span>X</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:2.4fr 1fr 1fr;"><span><strong>Total liabilities</strong></span><span><strong>X</strong></span><span><strong>X</strong></span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:2.4fr 1fr 1fr;"><span><strong>Net assets</strong></span><span><strong>X</strong></span><span><strong>X</strong></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:2.4fr 1fr 1fr;"><span><strong>NET ASSETS/EQUITY</strong></span><span></span><span></span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:2.4fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Contributed capital, reserves, accumulated surpluses/(deficits), NCI</span><span>X</span><span>X</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:2.4fr 1fr 1fr;"><span><strong>Total net assets/equity</strong></span><span><strong>X</strong></span><span><strong>X</strong></span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="grid grid-spaced">
      <div class="card cs">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Statement of Changes in Net Assets/Equity – condensed (para. 118–119)</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:2fr 1fr;"><span>Movement</span><span>Total</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:2fr 1fr;"><span>Balance at Dec 31, 20X0</span><span>X</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:2fr 1fr;"><span>Change in accounting policy</span><span>(X)</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:2fr 1fr;"><span><strong>Restated balance</strong></span><span><strong>X</strong></span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:2fr 1fr;"><span>Gain on property revaluation</span><span>X</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:2fr 1fr;"><span>Exchange differences on translation</span><span>(X)</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:2fr 1fr;"><span>Surplus for the period</span><span>X</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:2fr 1fr;"><span><strong>Balance at Dec 31, 20X1</strong></span><span><strong>X</strong></span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="note">Full illustration reconciles each component of net assets/equity (contributed capital, reserves, translation reserve, accumulated surpluses, NCI) from opening to closing balance, per para. 119(c).</div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Statement of Accounting Policies (Extract)</div>
        <div class="spec-box">
          <div class="spec-title">Reporting Entity</div>
          <div class="text-supporting">National government of Country A, per the Public Finance Act 20XX – comprising central government ministries and commercial public sector entities.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="spec-box">
          <div class="spec-title">Basis of Preparation</div>
          <div class="text-supporting">Complies with IPSAS for the accrual basis. Measurement base: historical cost adjusted for asset revaluations. Prepared on a going concern basis; policies applied consistently.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s20"><span class="num">20</span><h2>Statement / Process Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Concept-to-statement mapping table</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1.1fr 1.3fr;"><span>IPSAS 1 concept / requirement</span><span>Statement(s)</span><span>Notes</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1.1fr 1.3fr;"><span>Current/non-current or liquidity classification</span><span><span class="badge bs">SOFP</span></span><span>Structural choice, applied consistently</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1.1fr 1.3fr;"><span>By nature / by function expense presentation</span><span><span class="badge bp">SOFPerf</span></span><span>Policy choice; function needs extra note disclosure</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1.1fr 1.3fr;"><span>Revaluation surplus, defined-benefit remeasurements</span><span><span class="badge be">NA/E</span></span><span>Never reallocated to surplus/deficit</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1.1fr 1.3fr;"><span>FX translation, cash-flow-hedge reclassifications</span><span><span class="badge be">NA/E</span> <span class="badge bp">SOFPerf</span></span><span>Recycled on disposal/derecognition</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1.1fr 1.3fr;"><span>Dividends / distributions</span><span><span class="badge be">NA/E</span></span><span>A distribution, not a SOFPerf expense</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1.1fr 1.3fr;"><span>Budget-vs-actual comparison</span><span><span class="badge bc">Budget</span></span><span>Mandatory only if the budget is public (IPSAS 24)</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1.1fr 1.3fr;"><span>Cash flow statement itself</span><span><span class="badge bs">Cash Flow</span></span><span>The only statement not on the accrual basis (IPSAS 2)</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1.1fr 1.3fr;"><span>Going concern material uncertainty</span><span><span class="badge bp">Notes</span></span><span>Disclosed regardless of the conclusion reached</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1.1fr 1.3fr;"><span>Capital management &amp; puttable instruments</span><span><span class="badge bp">Notes</span></span><span>Paras. 148A–148D</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="grid grid-spaced">
      <div class="card cd">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Fair presentation: compliance &amp; departure decision</div>
        <ol class="ordered-list">
        <li>Apply IPSAS + necessary extra disclosure &rarr; PRESUMED fair presentation (para. 27)</li>
        <li>Would compliance mislead so badly it conflicts with the objective of financial statements? (extremely rare test, para. 31)</li>
        <li>Framework PERMITS departure &rarr; SHALL depart, disclosing the fair-presentation statement, the departed requirement, and financial impact per period</li>
        <li>Framework PROHIBITS departure &rarr; disclose the requirement, why it misleads, and the adjustments that would have achieved fair presentation</li>
      </ol>
        <div class="note">A departure driven purely by statutory/legislative requirements does not qualify for this process at all (para. 37) – that is simply non-compliance if material.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Current vs non-current liability – decision flow (post-2023)</div>
        <ol class="ordered-list">
        <li>Settlement due ≤12 months, normal operating cycle, or held for trading? &rarr; CURRENT (para. 80(a)–(c))</li>
        <li>Otherwise: does the entity have the RIGHT, at the reporting date, to defer settlement ≥12 months? (para. 80(d), 83A)</li>
        <li>Is that right subject to a covenant tested on/before the reporting date? If yes &rarr; affects the right (83B(a)). If tested only after &rarr; doesn't affect the right, but triggers disclosure (83B(b), 87A)</li>
        <li>Result: right exists &rarr; NON-CURRENT. No right (or already breached) &rarr; CURRENT, regardless of intent (86A)</li>
      </ol>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s21"><span class="num">21</span><h2>Related Standards – Cross-Reference Map</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">How IPSAS 1 connects across the IPSAS body of literature</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.7fr 2.2fr;"><span>Standard</span><span>Nature of the connection</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.7fr 2.2fr;"><span>IPSAS 2</span><span>Cash Flow Statements – governs the cash flow statement in full (§14) and cash-flow offsetting</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.7fr 2.2fr;"><span>IPSAS 3</span><span>Accounting Policies – policy-selection hierarchy (§6); comparative restatement for policy changes/errors (§9, §13)</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.7fr 2.2fr;"><span>IPSAS 4</span><span>Foreign Exchange Rates – presentation currency (§10); translation gains/losses routed outside surplus/deficit</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.7fr 2.2fr;"><span>IPSAS 12</span><span>Inventories – subclassification example on the SOFP; write-down disclosure trigger</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.7fr 2.2fr;"><span>IPSAS 14</span><span>Events after the Reporting Date – non-adjusting event disclosures for liability reclassifications (§11)</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.7fr 2.2fr;"><span>IPSAS 19</span><span>Provisions – provisions line item and offsetting example (§8, §10); contingent liability note ordering</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.7fr 2.2fr;"><span>IPSAS 24</span><span>Budget Information – mandatory budget-comparison component where the budget is public (§5)</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.7fr 2.2fr;"><span>IPSAS 28</span><span>Financial Instruments: Presentation – puttable-instrument terms (para. 7A); equity-vs-liability settlement options (§11, §16)</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.7fr 2.2fr;"><span>IPSAS 30</span><span>Financial Instruments: Disclosures – maturity-date disclosures; capital-management lineage, largely superseded by IPSAS 41</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.7fr 2.2fr;"><span>IPSAS 33</span><span>First-time Adoption – now the sole home for transitional relief IPSAS 1 formerly carried directly (§17)</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.7fr 2.2fr;"><span>IPSAS 34 / 35</span><span>Separate / Consolidated F/S – IPSAS 1 applies equally to both (§1); NCI presentation (§10)</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.7fr 2.2fr;"><span>IPSAS 39</span><span>Employee Benefits – function-of-expense additional disclosure (§12); remeasurement reserve type (§22)</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.7fr 2.2fr;"><span>IPSAS 41</span><span>Financial Instruments – reclassification adjustments, hedge-reserve items, FV-through-NA/E gains/losses (§10, §12, §13)</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.7fr 2.2fr;"><span>IPSAS 42</span><span>Social Benefits – social benefits liabilities line item &amp; subclassification by scheme (§10, §12)</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.7fr 2.2fr;"><span>IPSAS 44</span><span>Held for Sale &amp; Discontinued Operations – held-for-sale line items; discontinued-ops single line (§10, §12)</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.7fr 2.2fr;"><span>IPSAS 45</span><span>Property, Plant &amp; Equipment – measurement-model disaggregation driver; revaluation surplus reserve type</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.7fr 2.2fr;"><span>IPSAS 46 / 47</span><span>Measurement / Revenue – measurement-basis &amp; estimation-uncertainty guidance; revenue subclassification (§15, §12)</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s22"><span class="num">22</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary – Net Assets/Equity Reserve Types</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="three-col">
      <div class="card cn">
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Revaluation surplus</div><div class="glossary-def">IPSAS 31 (intangibles) / IPSAS 45 (PP&amp;E) – never reallocated to surplus/deficit; may transfer to accumulated surpluses as the asset is used or derecognized.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Defined-benefit remeasurements</div><div class="glossary-def">IPSAS 39 – never reallocated to surplus/deficit.</div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cn">
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Foreign operation translation reserve</div><div class="glossary-def">IPSAS 4 – reallocated to surplus/deficit on disposal of the foreign operation.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Cash flow hedge reserve</div><div class="glossary-def">IPSAS 41 – the effective portion; generally reallocated when the hedged item affects surplus/deficit.</div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cn">
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">FV through net assets/equity</div><div class="glossary-def">IPSAS 41 – equity instruments: never reallocated. Debt instruments: reallocated on derecognition.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Own-credit-risk reserve</div><div class="glossary-def">IPSAS 41 – fair-value changes attributable to changes in a designated liability's own credit risk.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

